  "name": "Question 20",
  "problem": "Solve the inequality $-{x}^{2}>-x-12$ and express the solution set in interval notation.\n\n$x\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$",
  "worked_solution": "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the terms to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2} & >-x-12 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+x+12 & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y=-{x}^{2}+x+12$ lies **above** the $x$-axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+x+12 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-x-12) & =0 \\\\ -(x+3)(x-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x=-3$ and $x=4,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004892/assets/32e9a7babfbe9132a3a3e18c2f1efcbc3a33cd59bccc02099c7045b3fc39ee75.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving \n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}+x+12>0,\n$$\n\n \nwe look for the part of the parabola that is **above** the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $-3<x<4.$\n\nUsing interval notation, we write $x\\in (-3,4).$",
